# libcss: custom-property environments look names up through a hash index (patch 497)
# Generated 2026-11-28
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 496)
#
# Each element's custom-property environment (fixes1268b/c) is a flat
# array of bindings. css__cp_env_add scanned it once per definition --
# quadratic for a rule defining hundreds of custom properties (github's
# Primer and theguardian.com's root variable blocks) -- and
# css__cp_env_find scanned every ancestor level once per var(), each
# candidate a dash-normalising string compare (cp_name_equal). Simulator
# after patch 496: cp_name_equal under these two was ~18% of github's
# main-thread time, css__cp_env_add ~11-13% of guardian's.
#
# An environment with 8 or more own bindings now keeps an open-addressing
# index (item index + 1 per slot) over its bindings by NORMALISED name
# (FNV-1a after the same up-to-two-leading-dashes rule as cp_name_equal),
# regrown at half load; a probe hit is still confirmed by cp_name_equal.
# The index stays valid: an add only replaces a binding's name with an
# equal one, and nothing reorders or removes bindings. Without an index
# (fewer bindings, or no memory) the scan answers, as before.
# cp_name_equal also returns at once for the same (interned) string.
#
# Verified exact before landing: a VITA_CPENV_CHECK build answered every
# add and find both ways -- 0 mismatches in 12.8 million lookups over the
# 21 census sites (MDN among them) and 12 custom-property test pages (stackoverflow
# 6.4M, oldreddit 2.9M, claude.ai 2.7M, guardian 436k). The check code
# stays behind that flag.
